{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The current front is dominated by the `iterative` family at the low-area end (7313 um^2), but `iterative` is a single-result-per-cycle architecture that is inherently area-inefficient compared to pipelined designs for high throughput. The `pipelined` family (1 result/cycle, N+2 latency) has not been explored yet. Given the constraint `throughput_msps >= 1` and the objective `min area_um2`, `pipelined` is the strongest candidate to beat the current best area of 7313 um^2. Pipelined architectures typically have lower area than unrolled or iterative designs for the same accuracy because they avoid the complex FSM/multiplexing of iterative/unrolled designs and can use simpler datapaths. The `pipelined_m` family (register every m stages) is already on the front but at much higher areas (25k-31k um^2), suggesting that full pipelining (`pipelined`, m=1 effectively) might offer a better area/accuracy trade-off if the datapath is optimized. We should explore `pipelined` to see if it can push the area below 7313 um^2 while maintaining accuracy >= 10 bits and throughput >= 1 MSPS.",
